{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"pwd && sed -n '1,220p' literature_review.md\"","aggregated_output":"/workspaces/reasoning-trace-robustnes-318e-codex\n# Literature Review: Reasoning Trace Length as a Proxy for Robustness\n\n## Review Scope\n\n### Research Question\nHow does reasoning trace length affect OOD robustness in LLM reasoning, and can adaptive trace-length control outperform fixed-length strategies?\n\n### Inclusion Criteria\n- Studies on chain-of-thought, trace control, process supervision, verifier-guided reasoning, or test-time reasoning compute.\n- Studies reporting reasoning benchmark results (GSM8K, MATH, BBH, ARC-like transfers).\n- OOD/distribution-shift analyses or efficiency-accuracy tradeoff analyses.\n\n### Exclusion Criteria\n- Purely non-LLM symbolic systems without relevance to modern LLM trace control.\n- Papers lacking empirical evidence or clear methodological detail.\n\n### Time Frame\n2022-2025 (plus foundational context where required).\n\n### Sources\n- arXiv API (primary)\n- Semantic Scholar API (supplementary)\n- Paper-finder script attempted but service timed out in this run\n\n## Search Log\n\n| Date | Query | Source | Results | Notes |\n|------|-------|--------|---------|-------|\n| 2026-04-06 | \"Reasoning Trace Length as a Proxy for Robustness ...\" | paper-finder script | timeout | Fallback to manual APIs |\n| 2026-04-06 | chain of thought length / OOD reasoning / test-time compute | arXiv API | 100+ candidates | deduplicated and ranked |\n| 2026-04-06 | CoT robustness / OOD generalization | Semantic Scholar API | merged into candidate pool | used for citation/context |\n\n## Screening Results\n\n- Title/abstract screened: 101\n- Full PDFs downloaded: 16\n- Deep-read (all chunk PDFs):\n  - `Let’s Verify Step by Step` (10 chunks)\n  - `Multi-Step Deductive Reasoning... OOD Generalisation` (6 chunks)\n  - `Is CoT a Mirage?` (13 chunks)\n\n## Key Papers\n\n### 1) Chain-of-Thought Prompting Elicits Reasoning in Large Language Models (2022)\n- **Key Contribution**: Establishes CoT prompting as a major reasoning boost.\n- **Methodology**: Few-shot CoT exemplars.\n- **Datasets**: Standard reasoning benchmarks (math/commonsense).\n- **Results**: Large gains vs direct answer prompting.\n- **Relevance**: Defines the baseline trace-length intervention.\n\n### 2) Self-Consistency Improves Chain of Thought Reasoning in Language Models (2022)\n- **Key Contribution**: Replaces greedy single-trace decoding with multi-trace sampling + voting.\n- **Methodology**: Sample diverse traces; marginalize answers.\n- **Datasets**: GSM8K, SVAMP, AQuA, StrategyQA, ARC-style sets (per paper).\n- **Results**: Stronger reasoning accuracy with added test-time compute.\n- **Relevance**: Central fixed-compute/variable-trace baseline for this project.\n\n### 3) Tree of Thoughts (2023)\n- **Key Contribution**: Search over thought states instead of pure left-to-right generation.\n- **Methodology**: Deliberate branching/evaluation/selection.\n- **Results**: Improved outcomes on planning/search-heavy tasks.\n- **Relevance**: Natural adaptive-length/control baseline.\n\n### 4) Let’s Verify Step by Step (2023)\n- **Key Contribution**: Process supervision outperforms outcome supervision.\n- **Methodology**: PRM vs ORM; step-level human labels (PRM800K), best-of-N search evaluation.\n- **Datasets Used**: MATH + held-out STEM OOD tests (AP Physics/Calculus/Chemistry, AMC10/12).\n- **Results**: Deep-read excerpts indicate PRM reaches ~78% solved on representative MATH subset and consistently beats ORM/majority-vote baselines; better OOD behavior than ORM.\n- **Relevance**: Direct evidence that *quality and selection* of traces matters more than raw verbosity.\n\n### 5) Chain-of-Thought Hub (2023)\n- **Key Contribution**: Open benchmark stack for reasoning models and prompts.\n- **Methodology**: Unified evaluation across GSM8K/MATH/BBH/MMLU etc.\n- **Relevance**: Practical infrastructure for controlled trace-length experiments.\n\n### 6) Math-Shepherd (2023)\n- **Key Contribution**: Automatic process reward model training without expensive human labeling.\n- **Relevance**: Scalable process-verifier signal for adaptive trace filtering.\n\n### 7) Contrastive Chain-of-Thought Prompting (2023)\n- **Key Contribution**: Uses negative reasoning examples to reduce typical CoT mistakes.\n- **Relevance**: Supports the view that trace content quality, not just length, drives robustness.\n\n### 8) GRACE (2023)\n- **Key Contribution**: Step-level correctness discriminator guides decoding.\n- **Relevance**: Verifier-guided adaptive pruning strategy.\n\n### 9) OCEAN (2024)\n- **Key Contribution**: Offline CoT evaluation/alignment with external structured feedback.\n- **Relevance**: Candidate offline evaluator for selecting robust trace regimes.\n\n### 10) Multi-Step Deductive Reasoning Over Natural Language (2022)\n- **Key Contribution**: Dedicated empirical OOD study for multi-step reasoning.\n- **Methodology**: IMA-GloVe-GA vs baselines on PARARULES/CONCEPTRULES; controlled OOD splits.\n- **Results**: Reported improvements over RNN baselines and stronger OOD behavior in deeper settings.\n- **Relevance**: Provides explicit OOD protocols transferable to LLM trace-length studies.\n\n### 11) Chain-of-Thought Reasoning is a Policy Improvement Operator (2023)\n- **Key Contribution**: Frames CoT as iterative policy improvement/self-learning.\n- **Relevance**: Theoretical rationale for adaptive iteration depth.\n\n### 12) Buffer of Thoughts (2024)\n- **Key Contribution**: Reusable thought templates for accuracy/efficiency/robustness.\n- **Relevance**: Structured trace compression and reuse; relevant to avoiding over-verbose traces.\n\n### 13) Is Chain-of-Thought Reasoning of LLMs a Mirage? A Data Distribution Lens (2025)\n- **Key Contribution**: Distribution-lens explanation of CoT success/failure.\n- **Methodology**: Controlled DataAlchemy setup with task/length/format shifts.\n- **Results**: Deep-read excerpts show strong in-distribution performance but severe degradation under composition, length, and format OOD; reports accuracy collapse under increased complexity.\n- **Relevance**: Strongest direct support for non-monotonic trace-length/robustness behavior.\n\n### 14) Slim-SC (2025)\n- **Key Contribution**: Thought pruning for efficient self-consistency.\n- **Relevance**: Explicit adaptive compute/length method to compare against fixed-k self-consistency.\n\n### 15) Sleep-time Compute (2025)\n- **Key Contribution**: Moves part of test-time reasoning compute offline.\n- **Relevance**: Alternative compute-allocation baseline when runtime trace expansion is expensive.\n\n### 16) Long CoT Survey (2025)\n- **Key Contribution**: Consolidates long-CoT methods, risks, and open problems.\n- **Relevance**: Context for trace-length scaling limits and safety/faithfulness issues.\n\n## Common Methodologies\n- Prompting-based trace elicitation: CoT few-shot/zero-shot and variants.\n- Sample-and-select: self-consistency, best-of-N reranking, verifier-guided selection.\n- Search-based inference: Tree-of-Thoughts/branching frameworks.\n- Process reward modeling: step-level supervision signals for filtering/reranking.\n- Controlled OOD probes: synthetic/compositional splits and cross-domain benchmarks.\n\n## Standard Baselines\n- Direct answer (no CoT)\n- Fixed CoT prompt with greedy decoding\n- Self-consistency with fixed sample count `k`\n- Majority voting over sampled traces\n- Outcome-supervised reward models (ORM)\n- Process-supervised reward models (PRM)\n\n## Evaluation Metrics\n- Primary: exact-match accuracy / problem solved rate\n- Robustness: in-distribution vs OOD delta, worst-group accuracy\n- Efficiency: tokens generated, latency, cost per correct answer\n- Selection quality: verifier agreement, pass@k / best-of-N curves\n\n## Datasets in the Literature\n- GSM8K, MATH/MATH-500, BBH, ARC-style QA\n- OOD-focused synthetic/compositional datasets (PARARULES, CONCEPTRULES, DataAlchemy-style controls)\n\n## Gaps and Opportunities\n- Limited direct measurement of **trace length as a continuous treatment variable** under fixed model weights.\n- Many works confound length with decoding strategy or model scale.\n- OOD robustness studies often focus on answer accuracy, not trace faithfulness/consistency jointly.\n- Adaptive stopping/pruning policies are promising but undercompared against strong fixed-length baselines across multiple task families.\n\n## Recommendations for Our Experiment\n- **Recommended datasets**: GSM8K, MATH-500, ARC-Challenge, BBH subset tasks, plus a controlled synthetic OOD generator.\n- **Recommended baselines**: direct answer, fixed CoT (short/medium/long), self-consistency (k={3,5,10,20}), ToT-style search, PRM/ORM reranking when available.\n- **Recommended metrics**: ID/OOD accuracy, robustness gap, token-cost efficiency, calibration/error-consistency under shift.\n- **Methodological considerations**:\n  - Sweep trace budgets at fixed model and prompt.\n  - Separate effects of verbosity from selection quality.\n  - Add uncertainty-triggered adaptive length control and compare to fixed policies at equal token budgets.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
